This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. This development builds upon previous work by Ambrozov\'{a}, Biehler, and Radaoui, who identified solutions for a related equation lacking the symmetry constraint, and our approach also addresses the case when $\Delta u$ is bounded. Additionally, we demonstrate the regularity of an associated set within the Sobolev space and provide a characterization of this set in the situation when $\Delta u$ is bounded, exhibiting a nontrivial, closed subspace of harmonic functions. The novel application of the homotopy-based minimax principle allows for a broader class of problems to be investigated. The introduced methods may also be applicable to other types of equations and problems. The research is supported by the German Academic Exchange Service (DAAD). The authors would like to extend their gratitude to the University of Oulu for providing access to necessary facilities. The research is also funded by the Academy of Finland through the projects 288403, 288424, 313415, and 320279. The research is further supported by the National Science Foundation under grant DMS-1912860. The research is also partially funded by the GNAMIT-NAN project of the Istituto Nazionale di Alta Matematica "Francesco Severi". The research is also supported by the Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia (FCT) through the project PD/BD/55292/2021. The research is also partially funded by the CNPq-Brazil through the project 307275/2020

